{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "prop:diffusion-fitted-center-corollary",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "In the additional subregime \\(T_N/N\\to0\\), uniformly over \\(\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{fit}}\\),\n\\[\n \\sqrt{NT_N}(\\overline\\theta_N-\\theta_N)\\Rightarrow\\mathcal N(0,Q),\n \\qquad\n \\mathbb P\\{\\theta_N\\in I_{\\mathrm{diff}}\\}=1-\\alpha+o(1).\n\\]\nMoreover \\(\\overline\\theta_N-\\widetilde\\theta_N=O_{\\mathbb P}(N^{-1})\\) uniformly.  Thus the diffusion fitted center is an \\(O(N^{-1})\\)-accurate corollary, and the available residual bound makes \\(T_N/N\\to0\\) sufficient for its Wald limit; this restriction is not imposed on exact finite-chain fitted-center inference.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "TN",
      "N",
      "Mfit",
      "thetadiffN",
      "thetaN",
      "Qproj",
      "Idiff",
      "alpha",
      "thetatildeN"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:fitted-horizon",
      "ass:test-size",
      "def:fitted-center",
      "def:wald-intervals",
      "thm:kink-residual-bound",
      "thm:fitted-center-wald"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(p'\\in[c/2,2C]^3\\), \\(1\\leq M\\leq N-1\\), and put\n\\[\n g'=\\frac{M-Na(p')}{\\sqrt N}.\n\\]\nThe control invariant mean divided by \\(N\\) is exactly \\(a_0(p')\\).  By the definition of the scaled treatment state, its invariant mean divided by \\(N\\) is\n\\[\n a(p')+N^{-1/2}\\mathbb E_{\\rho_{1,N}^{p',M}}X_{1,N}^{p',M}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n \\begin{split}\n &\\vartheta_N(p';M)-\n \\{a_0(p')-a(p')-N^{-1/2}m_{p',g'}\\}\\\\\n &\\qquad=-N^{-1/2}\n \\{\\mathbb E_{\\rho_{1,N}^{p',M}}X_{1,N}^{p',M}-m_{p',g'}\\}. \\tag{1}\n \\end{split}\n\\]\n\nApply Theorem \\(\\mathrm{kink\\mbox{-}residual\\mbox{-}bound}\\) with the compact rate bounds \\(c/2,2C\\) and staffing radius \\(G+1\\).  Its enrollment threshold is finite, so \\(N\\to\\infty\\) eventually exceeds it.  The centering consequence proved there turns (1) into\n\\[\n \\left|\\vartheta_N(p';M)-\n \\{a_0(p')-a(p')-N^{-1/2}m_{p',g'}\\}\\right|\n \\leq K N^{-1}                                                \\tag{2}\n\\]\nuniformly whenever \\(|g'|\\leq G+1\\).\n\nThe rate-score coordinate of Theorem \\(\\mathrm{uniform\\mbox{-}joint\\mbox{-}law}\\) gives\n\\[\n \\widehat p-p=O_{\\mathbb P}((NT_N)^{-1/2}).                   \\tag{3}\n\\]\nSmoothness of \\(a\\) on the compact positive rate box yields\n\\[\n \\widehat\\gamma_N-\\gamma_N\n =-\\sqrt N\\{a(\\widehat p)-a(p)\\}=O_{\\mathbb P}(T_N^{-1/2}),\n \\qquad 0\\leq\\gamma_N-\\gamma<N^{-1/2}.                       \\tag{4}\n\\]\nSince \\(T_N\\to\\infty\\), the event \\(\\widehat p\\in[c/2,2C]^3\\) and \\(|\\widehat\\gamma_N|\\leq G+1\\) has probability tending to one uniformly.  Take \\(p'=\\widehat p,M=M_N\\) in (2) on this event.  Definition \\(\\mathrm{fitted\\mbox{-}center}\\) then gives\n\\[\n \\overline\\theta_N-\\widetilde\\theta_N=O_{\\mathbb P}(N^{-1}) \\tag{5}\n\\]\nuniformly.  Under \\(T_N/N\\to0\\),\n\\[\n \\sqrt{NT_N}(\\overline\\theta_N-\\widetilde\\theta_N)\n =O_{\\mathbb P}(\\sqrt{T_N/N})=o_{\\mathbb P}(1).               \\tag{6}\n\\]\nTheorem \\(\\mathrm{fitted\\mbox{-}center\\mbox{-}wald}\\) gives\n\\[\n \\sqrt{NT_N}(\\widetilde\\theta_N-\\theta_N)\n \\Rightarrow\\mathcal N(0,Q),\n \\qquad \\widehat Q/Q\\to1,                                    \\tag{7}\n\\]\nuniformly, with \\(Q\\) bounded away from zero.  Equations (6)--(7) prove\n\\[\n \\sqrt{NT_N}(\\overline\\theta_N-\\theta_N)\n \\Rightarrow\\mathcal N(0,Q).\n\\]\nStudentization by \\(\\widehat Q\\), \\(\\alpha\\in(0,1)\\), and integration of the normal density over \\([-z_\\alpha,z_\\alpha]\\) yield\n\\[\n \\sup_{p\\in\\mathcal P,\\,|\\gamma|\\leq G}\n \\left|\\mathbb P_{p,\\gamma}\\{\\theta_N\\in I_{\\mathrm{diff}}\\}-(1-\\alpha)\\right|\n \\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nEquation (5) proves the claimed \\(O_{\\mathbb P}(N^{-1})\\) approximation, and (6) isolates the sole use of the fitted-horizon condition.",
    "justification": "This corollary isolates the sole use of the fitted-horizon condition.",
    "gap": "It quantifies the price of replacing exact product-law centering by the diffusion mean.",
    "consumer": "Implementers may choose the cheaper diffusion center when monitoring duration grows more slowly than enrollment.",
